Environment: evo-flight-lab:sealed, linux/arm64, network none.
Command: COMPILER -std=gnu11 -OLEVEL volatile-bit-fields.c -o /tmp/fields;
         /tmp/fields
gcc-14 O0: exit=0
gcc-14 O2: exit=0
clang-19 O0: exit=0
clang-19 O2: exit=0

O2 assembly inspection, not a claim of identical instruction sequences:
read_small: both one 32-bit load.
write_small: GCC one 16-bit load/store; Clang one 32-bit load/store.
increment_small: GCC 32-bit load + 16-bit load/store;
                 Clang two 32-bit loads + one 32-bit store.
read_large: both one 64-bit load.
write_large: both one 64-bit load/store.
read_three (packed size 3): GCC three byte loads;
                           Clang byte load at 2 + halfword load at 0.
write_three: GCC three byte read/modify/write accesses;
             Clang halfword and byte loads, then halfword and byte stores.

The agreed numerical results do not establish a single oracle access trace.
The reviewed declared-container rule chooses 32 bits for the small ordinary
record, as Clang does; packed accesses must stay within their storage span.
